Two more variants of this, at main @ f6f47d181. In the first, the wrong order makes the interpreter crash with a Go interface-conversion error rather than just producing a wrong value.

```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

class C {
    function get() returns int {
        return 9;
    }
}

int n = f();
int[] xs = [1, 2, 3];

int m = g();
C c = new;

function f() returns int {
    return xs.length();
}

function g() returns int {
    return c.get();
}

public function main() {
    io:println(n); // @output 3
    io:println(m); // @output 9
}
```

Actual:
```
error: interface conversion: values.BalValue is nil, not *values.Object
        at g(bug-module-var-init-fn-dep-v.bal:20)
           init(bug-module-var-init-fn-dep-v.bal:12)
```
Expected: `3` then `9`. Without the `m`/`c` part, `n` prints `0`: `xs.length()` ran on an uninitialised (nil) list. With a record (`R r = {x: 5}` read through a function), it crashes with `unsupported type combination: <nil> + int64`. A `string` read this way becomes `""`.

A cycle through a function body is also not diagnosed:
```ballerina
int a = f();
function f() returns int { return a + 1; }
```
This prints `1` with no error, when an initialization cycle error is expected. The fix belongs in `dependencyVisitor` / `toplogicallySortInits` in `desugar/desugar.go`, which only collects direct variable references and does not follow called functions (see also #1322 and #1483).

